A||||17||Gerald||2.0 turbo ?????||||1||
Z||000000||Gerald||10-06-2001||01:37 AM||||I was looking thru the archives and stumbled across a topic about 2.0 turbos swaped into fieros. Can anyone tell me about this. How hard is the swap. I have a 2.5 right now.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||10-06-2001||02:01 AM||||it was done as a dealer option when they were new forget the name they used.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||10-06-2001||02:18 AM||||I can't help with the swap but here are some specs. 2.0 OHC Turbo was rated at 165 hp. at 5600 rpm and 175 lb-ft of torque at 4000. Redline of 6400 rpm. Turbo is a Garett T2.5 water cooled producing 9 lbs of boost controled by a wastegate. Both piston and rods are forged, and the engine has a windage tray/crank scraper for oil control.<P>If you do the swap it should easily outrun a 2.8. The engine was available in both the Sunbird GT and Grand Am SE in 88.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gerald||10-06-2001||11:50 AM||||Cool, I got to find that engine. Any idea on how much Ill be paying?||||reg||1
Z||000004||SKIDMARK||10-06-2001||12:11 PM||||A better swap would be the DOHC quad 4 from a early 90's Grand Am. It's a better engine with more HP and torque and probably easier to find. Do some research first. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||10-06-2001||12:15 PM||||It was called the Delta 204T option also known as the Andretti Special. It was a teaming of GM and a company called Red Shift Ltd in Oregon. There were 3 dealers that offered it and only 11 were made. It had a claimed 0-60 time of 6.56 seconds with the 3.18 automatic.<BR>    I would believe that even a mild 3.4 would eat this configuration for lunch.||||reg||6
Z||000006||ray b||10-06-2001||12:26 PM||||turbo and auto's do not work well togetter.<BR>esp for off the line drags,0-60, better, in top end and is lighter so will handle better.<BR>try a search on the net for junk-yards prices<BR>one site had a list of many dealers listing all u s motors + condision/miles/runing/compressionchecks ect.<BR>lost it in a crash!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||10-06-2001||08:54 PM||||"turbo and auto's do not work well togetter."<P>For godsake, don't let the GN/TR/SyTy people hear you say that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I feel just the opposite. The auto allows the shift points to be optimized. Spooling just needs some thought and a matching torque converter.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rick 88||10-06-2001||11:16 PM||||The turbo 2.0 was much better suited to a 5-speed due to the small displacement. An 88 5.7 auto GTA Trans Am turned a 15.8 quarter stock. The Sunbird turbo 5-speed did the same thing in 15.9. Not too shabby considering the difference in displacement. These figures are directly from the Pontiac <BR>"Hot Ones" factory brochure in 88. <P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gerald||10-07-2001||05:42 AM||||Ive heard really bad things about the quad 4 swap. Good power but hard to do. I also heard that the reliabelity of the 2.0 is great. If I cant find a turbo Im just gonna swap a N/A in. Its got to be more capable then the duke and it has a way higher redline. Im going to start looking around tomorrow for a engine. Hope to find one for less then 300. Is that feasible?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wannabe||10-07-2001||03:33 PM||||Every Turbo Sunfire I've seen lately is a derelict with no hope of finding a good home. There's one just down the street for sale - it's been sitting on their driveway with a for sale sign for about 6 months now - you should have no probem getting one cheap. Assuming the engine is in good shape or can be properly rebuilt, there's no reason why you shouldn't be able to see 200-225hp with an intercooler and some more boost. From what I recall, it was also a reasonable lightweight package.||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||10-07-2001||06:56 PM||||I've heard the same thing. The 1.8 turbo's were a bit goofy, but the 2.0L had all of the needed improvements. I've always thought about playing with one. <P>I won't dispute that the small turbo engines work better with the manuals. The Sunbird Turbo's I've driven were all manuals but they were a blast to drive.||||reg||14
Z||000012||Gerald||10-07-2001||08:58 PM||||While doing the clutch in my car today ( and yesterday) i relized that my 2.5 is really bad shape. I cant possible begin to try and find all the leaks. It has low compression on cyl 2 and is just plain slow. I got beat by a camary. How sad is that. Id say the only good thing about the drivetrain is the clutch that i havent even tried yet. Its in there but I havent drove it yet. If anyone knows of a 2.0 turbo in the Edmonton area give me a shout.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Areakon||10-07-2001||11:37 PM||||I have a 87 sunbird Se that had the 2.0L turbo engine. Before I got it the wastegate got stuck and blew the engine to pieces. So I bought it off my sisters husband after he rebuilt it. He never put a turbo back on it though. Without the turbo it is a zippy engine for 2.0 liters it is pretty strong. The only problem I've head is the top end. The car drove fine for the first 8,000 miles. Then the head gasket went out. So I replaced it. I then drove it for another 2,000 miles when the head decided to crack. So I put another head on it and drove it till the tranny went out in July. I've also talked to a lot of other people that have had similar problems. In my opinion I would stay away from the 2.0L unless you like changing the top end. <P>------------------<BR>He who knows nothing, loves nothing. He who can do nothing understands nothing. He who understands nothing is worthless.<BR>              Paracelsus||||reg||1
Z||000014||erikred||10-08-2001||10:35 AM||||I considered this myself. I still might do it in the future. My local junk yard will sell me a running complete motor for $400.<P>The starter is on the wrong side of the block and would have to be changed to the other side some how. Also you will need the computer and wiring harness from the same car. I have the turbo off of one of these cars right now. I am going to put it on my Duke after I rebuild it. Of course it woulbe cheaper to go ahead and install the turbo engine though....<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000015||qwikgta||10-08-2001||09:10 PM||||This may be a dumb ?? and I have not looked into it at all.  Maybe one of you will know the answer to this.  I have read somewhere recently on the fourm that the duke engine is basicly half a pontiac 301, i understand what this means, but I was woundering if the exhaust manifold would fit.  In 79-81 Pontiac put a turbo on some 301 motors and i wounder if somehow the manifold that held the turbo would fit on a duke, maybe with some kind of adapter or maybe some new holes drilled into it, again, I was just thinking that this may work, I am pretty sure it will not, but it can't hurt to ask.<P>Rob ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Eric_PA||10-09-2001||09:46 AM||||I have been through many 2.0 OHC engines. 5 to be exact. 3 of them ended up having head gasket problems, and two had a bad cam. Those two problems are the most common for these engines, from what I have heard. I have one with 61k on it setting in the garage that ran really well, before my ex-girlfriend smashed the car. I was thinking about puting it in my 84 last year, but then decided not to. The starter is mounted on the other side of these also. They do run strong for 2 liters of displacement with 2 valves per cylinder.<P>Eric<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Gerald||10-09-2001||11:30 AM||||how would i go about switching the starter to the other side?||||reg||1
